  "query_preview": "..This morning's MCS has now turned into an MCV and\nis moving towards Puerto Penasco with isolated storms over the\nWhite Mountains, Chiricahuas, and Pinalenos. The atmosphere is \nvery moist with surface dewpoints in the low to mid 60s across\nsoutheast Arizona. PWATs are 1.3\" along the AZ/NM border, 1.5\" \nover Tucson, and 2\" over far western Pima County. Aloft broad\nhigh pressure covers most of the southern half of the country\nwith two centers over the AZ/NM border and the coast of South\nCarolina. This will put southeast Arizona under east to \nnortheast steering flow, allowing for a possible rim shot day. \nStorm movement will probably be more NE-SW coming off the rim \nand more east to west elsewhere. CAMs show continued development\nover the higher terrain east of Tucson as well as south through\nthe afternoon with storms then moving into the lower deserts \nlate afternoon into the evening. The last couple HRRR runs have \nkept Tucson on the northern periphery of storm coverage, with \nbest chances for Tucson seeing storms this evening. The biggest\nthreat today will be heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding \nwith the best chances for this south and east of Tucson. The \n09/12Z HREF is also hinting at a slight chance for isolated \nstrong to severe winds late this afternoon and evening over \nGraham and eastern Pinal Counties as storms move off the rim. \n\nHigh pressure stays centered to the west northwest over the next\ncouple of days keeping southeast Arizona under northeast flow.\nAbove normal moisture will still be in place leading to\ncontinued storm chances south and east of Tucson Thursday and\nFriday.\n\nThis weekend into early next week a closed low moves in the\nPacific Northwest and moves inland towards the northern Rockies.\nThis will shunt the ridge to the east over Texas or the\nsoutheast U.S. Mid-level flow may briefly turn southwest but for\nthe most part will stay southerly to easterly, which will allow\nmoisture to stick around. Expect continued daily isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms mainly from central Pima County eastward\nwith the main threat being heavy rainfall. As September \ncontinues the sun angle will continue to lower with less intense\nsurface hearing. This will result in a slight reduction of \nstorm intensity without more upper level support, even with \nabove normal moisture.",
